#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060851} :activity/id #uuid "2a764071-28cf-5711-b033-8d2e6a3fee8f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050668 :topic/id #uuid "5d74b293-aea3-563f-8c4c-f8febb13e3a8" :topic/math-academy-id 267} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060989 :tutorial/content "To multiply powers of the same base, we simply *add* the exponents. This is called the **product rule for exponents**.\nFor example, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3}).$ Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4}\\times {3}^{2} & = \\\\ {{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times {{3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{6\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3baed7a9-d08b-5c41-8d2c-77d4f4daf35d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8156 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3f48be73-d211-5343-8cf8-db9420dc3457" :step/math-academy-id 72192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059871 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059422 :question/id #uuid "3625f2a9-3103-551e-b350-b0c83d9b241f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13039"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "997d255f-89af-5563-b10f-eecaae62d8c0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049714}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480648} {:db/id #edb/ref 